<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Isometric scene camera for the web cockpit (ML3.5 F3b).

  Feeds the blue panel: the external view showing the robot MOVING. It is the
  only panel that answers "did the robot move?" without depending on a stack node.

  WHY THIS IS A SPAWNED MODEL INSTEAD OF A BLOCK INSIDE worlds/*.sdf
  The first version put both cameras directly in quadruped_empty.sdf and
  quadruped_maze11.sdf. That leaves the blue panel DEAD in the default setup:
  docker/compose.host.yml does not pass `world:=`, and sim.launch.py defaults to
  warehouse.sdf from nav2_minimal_tb4_sim, an installed package file that cannot
  be edited. A panel that works only in a non-default world is worse than no
  panel. As a spawned model, the same model works in ANY world, including the
  warehouse, with its pose supplied as a launch argument.

  And why not attach the camera to the robot: go2_description is vendored with
  a byte-for-byte guarantee that supports its licensing claim (see its README).
  Editing that package is prohibited. See decision 6 in
  docs/ml35/plano-cockpit-web.md.

  REQUIRES gz-sim-sensors-system TO BE IN THE WORLD. Without this plugin, the
  sensor exists but publishes nothing, and no log line names the missing plugin.
  Checked: warehouse.sdf, quadruped_empty.sdf, and the other project worlds all
  load the plugin.

  The pose comes from `create` (-x -y -z -R -P -Y), not from here. See
  launch/scene_cameras.launch.py for the values and framing calculations.

  1600x1200 a 10 Hz.

  CAUTION: 1200x900 was tried on 25/08/2026 and REVERTED without a conclusion.
  The reasoning still applies and is recorded below, but runs with that setting
  ended with the robot FALLING (31.6-degree tilt at t=62 s, and a second fall at
  startup with 26 supervisor RECOVER actions); "zero falls" is a V3 gate
  criterion. The fall was NOT attributed to this parameter: bench real-time
  factor had dropped to ~0.5 and stayed at 0.54 with perception stopped,
  pointing to accumulated bench state. Since the causes could not be separated,
  the validated value was restored. To revisit this: measure with a freshly
  restarted bench, n >= 3, and record the RTF for every run.

  The first version used 800x600 at 5 Hz because "the panel does not need more."
  It did: the scene panel is about 1200 px wide, so the 800 px sensor image was
  enlarged and maze walls — large, nearly uniform surfaces, the worst case for
  JPEG — arrived with dirty edges. 1600x1200 fixed the image and overshot what
  was needed: 1600 px on a 1200 px panel means downscaling, not added sharpness.

  1200x900 would match the sensor to the panel EXACTLY, meeting the same criterion
  that ruled out 800x600 (no upscaling) with 44% fewer pixels than 1600x1200.
  That is the direction for the next attempt. The useful rule is "sensor = panel
  width," not a decorative number.

  WHY PIXELS MATTER, AND WHY REAL-TIME FACTOR DOES NOT TELL THE WHOLE STORY.
  Measured on the bench on 25/08/2026, x86 host, maze11, nav_trial protocol
  (180 s, 90 s goal timeout), with perception stopped at both ends:

      scene cameras ON  ->  0.0155 m/s,  peak cmd_vx 0.012 m/s
      scene cameras OFF ->  0.0515 m/s,  peak cmd_vx 0.139 m/s

  Over 3x the average navigation speed, with REAL-TIME FACTOR AT 0.96 IN BOTH
  RUNS. That is why this header previously concluded that 10 Hz "cost almost
  nothing": RTF measured physics, not the sensor queue. The two RTF values below
  remain valid and remain insufficient as a criterion.

  When measuring navigation, turn off the cameras (scene_cameras:=false) or
  always compare runs with the cameras in the SAME state. Comparing different
  states creates an apparent regression where none occurred — that happened on
  25/08/2026, making the unchanged V3 tuning in the repository appear 15x worse.

  The 4:3 aspect ratio is DELIBERATE. The horizontal_fov and framing of both
  cameras were measured at this ratio (see scene_cameras.launch.py); switching
  to 16:9 while keeping the hfov would crop vertically and misframe both scenes.

  THE RATE IS 10 Hz, NOT 15, BASED ON MEASUREMENT, not caution. Each camera
  renders in the operator's Gazebo process, and at this resolution the
  workstation cannot deliver 15 Hz anyway. Measured on the bench, same world
  (maze11):

      update_rate 15  ->  9.4 Hz delivered, real-time factor 0.59 (at 1600x1200)
      update_rate 10  ->  9.8 Hz delivered, real-time factor 0.97 (at 1600x1200)

  In other words, requesting 15 delivered no extra frames and cost 40% of
  simulation speed — stretching every Nav2 goal by the same proportion. Measure
  again before raising this value. If the bottleneck is bandwidth rather than
  rendering (HIL mode, Ethernet to the Aquila), lower JPEG quality first in
  hmi/js/config.js; it degrades gracefully, unlike changing this resolution,
  which shifts the framing in pixels.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <model name="cockpit_scene_iso">
    <static>true</static>
    <link name="camera">
      <sensor name="scene_iso" type="camera">
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>1.20</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>1600</width>
            <height>1200</height>
          </image>
          <!-- 8 samples: maze wall edges are mostly diagonal, and diagonal
               aliasing is the most visible artifact in a static render. -->
          <anti_aliasing>8</anti_aliasing>
          <clip>
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>80</far>
          </clip>
          <camera_info_topic>cockpit/scene_iso/camera_info</camera_info_topic>
        </camera>
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <topic>cockpit/scene_iso/image</topic>
      </sensor>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
